1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,887
This repository has 39 commits,
and 33 of them carry the same message:

2
00:00:05,887 --> 00:00:07,232
/run-deploy-app.

3
00:00:07,552 --> 00:00:09,088
That sentence is a button.

4
00:00:09,408 --> 00:00:13,056
Pushed to main,
it rebuilds the app and redeploys a server.

5
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:15,732
And once, the slash leans the other way.

6
00:00:16,052 --> 00:00:18,688
To the workflow,
that is a different sentence,

7
00:00:18,688 --> 00:00:20,063
and nothing runs at all.

8
00:00:20,383 --> 00:00:21,492
One character.

9
00:00:21,733 --> 00:00:26,000
How does a sentence deploy a server,
and why did one character stop it?

10
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:29,605
By the end of part 2,
you'll push that sentence yourself.

11
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:34,991
This is the course's last lecture:
final deployment and review, in three parts.

12
00:00:35,311 --> 00:00:38,506
Its motivation
is a playful prediction web page;

13
00:00:38,506 --> 00:00:39,704
our reading of it:

14
00:00:39,704 --> 00:00:45,146
even a toy prediction app is only useful
when it runs somewhere, and stays current.

15
00:00:45,383 --> 00:00:48,754
This recording machine has no AWS account.

16
00:00:49,074 --> 00:00:51,378
Everything on the laptop ran for real.

17
00:00:51,698 --> 00:00:56,737
Every command that talks to AWS ran
exactly as the README writes it,

18
00:00:56,737 --> 00:00:58,738
up to its real first error.

19
00:00:59,058 --> 00:01:00,587
The consoles, the server

20
00:01:00,587 --> 00:01:03,453
and GitHub are drawings,
marked illustration.

21
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,539
You need Docker,
your own AWS account with billing,

22
00:01:07,539 --> 00:01:10,249
and for part 2, a GitHub repository.

23
00:01:10,569 --> 00:01:13,535
This lecture runs a server
until you delete it.

24
00:01:13,855 --> 00:01:18,015
The tracker names the README step,
and your turn means pause and run it.

25
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:20,965
Here is the lecture's map.

26
00:01:21,285 --> 00:01:23,293
The dev team commits to GitHub,

27
00:01:23,293 --> 00:01:26,377
GitHub Actions builds
and deploys to the cloud,

28
00:01:26,377 --> 00:01:28,346
and users reach the app there.

29
00:01:28,583 --> 00:01:31,997
Above it, the app as one chain,
from data to frontend.

30
00:01:32,233 --> 00:01:34,768
The slides speak Google Cloud;

31
00:01:34,768 --> 00:01:38,740
we follow the AWS repository,
as in lecture 15.

32
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:41,705
Now the same map in AWS words.

33
00:01:42,025 --> 00:01:47,209
The Kubernetes cluster becomes one
EC2 server running Docker containers.

34
00:01:47,529 --> 00:01:51,817
The image registry becomes ECR,
with three repositories.

35
00:01:52,137 --> 00:01:54,313
Pulumi becomes Ansible playbooks.

36
00:01:54,550 --> 00:02:01,035
The service account key becomes two secrets:
an IAM access key and an EC2 key pair.

37
00:02:01,355 --> 00:02:03,769
The keyword becomes /run-deploy-app,

38
00:02:03,769 --> 00:02:07,926
and the ingress becomes one nginx
container at the front door.

39
00:02:08,167 --> 00:02:10,895
There's no Kubernetes and no Pulumi here:

40
00:02:10,895 --> 00:02:14,887
one server, set up by Ansible,
and no machine learning lane.

41
00:02:15,133 --> 00:02:17,437
The lecture lists five things to automate.

42
00:02:17,757 --> 00:02:21,272
For this repo:
pipeline jobs and model training,

43
00:02:21,272 --> 00:02:23,176
no, those were lecture 15.

44
00:02:23,496 --> 00:02:27,080
Frontend and backend deployment,
yes, always together.

45
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:31,091
Building and pushing containers, yes:
three app images.

46
00:02:31,333 --> 00:02:32,508
The lecture's plan:

47
00:02:32,508 --> 00:02:36,773
we already have a deployment container,
so let GitHub Actions run it.

48
00:02:37,093 --> 00:02:39,184
Here it's called cheese-app-deployment.

49
00:02:39,433 --> 00:02:42,974
The slides say:
invoke the command line in that container.

50
00:02:43,294 --> 00:02:45,854
Here, that means ansible-playbook commands.

51
00:02:46,174 --> 00:02:50,847
In part 1 you type them;
in part 2, they arrive as one long command.

52
00:02:52,083 --> 00:02:55,880
These first commands are extra;
the README has no clone step.

53
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:01,790
One branch, unchanged since we pinned it,
and inside src, the deployment folder.

54
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:05,809
The list of APIs
to enable sits inside a comment;

55
00:03:05,809 --> 00:03:08,582
on AWS there's nothing to switch on.

56
00:03:08,902 --> 00:03:13,404
One check:
the API calls Bedrock models in us-east-1,

57
00:03:13,404 --> 00:03:18,246
chosen in 2024,
so check your account can still use them.

58
00:03:18,483 --> 00:03:23,112
Step 2, in the IAM console:
create a policy from this JSON.

59
00:03:23,432 --> 00:03:27,282
It allows one identity check
and nine ECR actions,

60
00:03:27,282 --> 00:03:30,515
to log in, push and pull, on any resource.

61
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:35,480
Name it CheeseAppECRPolicy,
and pause to create it;

62
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:38,475
an extra check shows the JSON parses.

63
00:03:38,795 --> 00:03:42,592
It can't create a repository,
so you'll make three by hand later.

64
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:47,057
Step 3: create a user,
deployment-ac215-user.

65
00:03:47,377 --> 00:03:53,969
It gets three policies AWS manages:
full access to S3, EC2 and Bedrock.

66
00:03:54,289 --> 00:03:56,422
And your own ECR policy.

67
00:03:56,666 --> 00:04:00,635
Step 4: create an access key
for a third party service,

68
00:04:00,635 --> 00:04:02,511
and download the CSV file.

69
00:04:02,831 --> 00:04:05,656
Save it in a secrets folder
beside your clone,

70
00:04:05,656 --> 00:04:08,357
not inside it, so it can never be committed.

71
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:13,635
Step 5: create an EC2 key pair,
and save its pem file there too.

72
00:04:13,955 --> 00:04:20,248
The badge talks to the AWS API;
the key opens the SSH door of your server.

73
00:04:20,483 --> 00:04:22,574
An extra check shows a small gap.

74
00:04:22,894 --> 00:04:27,246
The README names the key pair
cheese-app-ac215-key-pair.

75
00:04:27,566 --> 00:04:32,920
The code expects ac215_2024, in three files.

76
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:40,174
Easiest fix: use that name,
so the file is ac215_2024.pem.

77
00:04:40,416 --> 00:04:43,317
Extra: chmod 400 on the pem file.

78
00:04:43,637 --> 00:04:47,328
SSH refuses a private key
that others can open.

79
00:04:49,467 --> 00:04:53,136
Step 6, deployment setup, brings a new tool:
Ansible.

80
00:04:53,456 --> 00:04:57,360
Pulumi, in lecture 20,
described the infrastructure you want.

81
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:02,566
Ansible runs an ordered list of tasks
against the machines in an inventory.

82
00:05:02,886 --> 00:05:05,398
Many of its modules describe a state;

83
00:05:05,398 --> 00:05:08,454
this repo also runs
plenty of shell commands.

84
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:10,577
A playbook is a list of plays.

85
00:05:10,897 --> 00:05:15,825
Each play names its hosts and holds tasks,
and each task calls one module.

86
00:05:16,145 --> 00:05:21,974
The hosts line decides where tasks run:
localhost means inside your toolbox;

87
00:05:21,974 --> 00:05:25,042
appserver means on the server, over SSH.

88
00:05:25,284 --> 00:05:27,780
Here is that inventory, as one file.

89
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,959
Ansible logs in as the user ubuntu.

90
00:05:31,279 --> 00:05:34,538
Line 4 points at the key for GitHub Actions;

91
00:05:34,538 --> 00:05:38,169
line 7, commented out,
at the key on your laptop.

92
00:05:38,489 --> 00:05:42,189
Lines 9 to 11 take your keys
from the environment:

93
00:05:42,189 --> 00:05:43,374
nothing to type.

94
00:05:43,617 --> 00:05:48,396
Then the machine: the region, us-east-1,
and a t2.large instance.

95
00:05:48,716 --> 00:05:50,934
The key pair name, marked change this.

96
00:05:51,254 --> 00:05:55,713
An Ubuntu machine image for that region,
and the disk names and sizes.

97
00:05:55,950 --> 00:05:59,107
The README's four bullets point
at exactly these lines.

98
00:05:59,427 --> 00:06:03,854
Change two now: the key pair name,
unless yours already matches,

99
00:06:03,854 --> 00:06:08,281
and line 24, the NEXTAUTH_SECRET,
to a random value of your own.

100
00:06:08,601 --> 00:06:10,820
The host on line 30 comes later.

101
00:06:13,584 --> 00:06:15,498
Every run ends with a recap;

102
00:06:15,498 --> 00:06:18,917
success means
failed and unreachable stay at zero.

103
00:06:19,237 --> 00:06:23,205
A declarative task run twice
reports ok the second time.

104
00:06:25,950 --> 00:06:28,382
Step 1: go to the deployment folder.

105
00:06:28,702 --> 00:06:32,798
Here are the playbooks,
the inventory and docker-shell.sh.

106
00:06:33,033 --> 00:06:34,249
Open the script.

107
00:06:34,569 --> 00:06:38,430
It splits the second line of your CSV
into two variables.

108
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:43,807
Line 17 hard codes the account
ID of the author, a placeholder here.

109
00:06:44,050 --> 00:06:47,741
Run it as cloned, and it stops:
credentials file not found.

110
00:06:48,061 --> 00:06:53,309
Four ../ from the deployment folder end
at /home, one level too far.

111
00:06:53,550 --> 00:06:57,817
Step 2 is that fix:
three levels up, with sed or your editor.

112
00:06:58,137 --> 00:06:59,630
Now the file is found.

113
00:07:03,383 --> 00:07:07,735
Step 3:
sh docker-shell.sh, exactly as written.

114
00:07:08,055 --> 00:07:10,818
Surprise 1, on this Ubuntu laptop:

115
00:07:10,818 --> 00:07:14,882
a syntax error on line 13,
redirection unexpected.

116
00:07:15,117 --> 00:07:19,234
Extra: on Ubuntu, sh is dash,
which has no here-string (<<<).

117
00:07:19,554 --> 00:07:23,266
And typing sh skips the first line,
which asks for bash.

118
00:07:23,586 --> 00:07:27,447
On a Mac the README line works;
on Linux, use bash instead.

119
00:07:27,683 --> 00:07:31,367
Surprise 2, at the time of recording,
on our machine:

120
00:07:31,367 --> 00:07:33,592
the build stops at step 9 of 15.

121
00:07:33,912 --> 00:07:38,329
Curl can't verify the Helm download site,
so no signing key arrives.

122
00:07:38,567 --> 00:07:42,067
And the script doesn't stop:
set -e is commented out,

123
00:07:42,067 --> 00:07:44,775
so it runs an image that was never built.

124
00:07:45,095 --> 00:07:47,868
Check the build output,
not just the last line.

125
00:07:48,117 --> 00:07:50,570
Our machine didn't trust
that certificate's root.

126
00:07:50,890 --> 00:07:52,149
Your laptop may.

127
00:07:52,469 --> 00:07:56,852
No playbook uses Helm,
so delete these two lines and the word helm,

128
00:07:56,852 --> 00:07:58,357
and the toolbox builds.

129
00:08:04,150 --> 00:08:08,225
Surprise 3: inside the toolbox,
/secrets is empty.

130
00:08:08,545 --> 00:08:13,878
Line 26 has the same four ../,
so Docker mounted an empty folder.

131
00:08:14,198 --> 00:08:16,609
The prompt ID changes on every start.

132
00:08:16,850 --> 00:08:22,674
So fix line 26 like line 10,
and put your own account ID on line 17.

133
00:08:22,994 --> 00:08:27,837
aws sts get-caller-identity shows it,
once your keys work.

134
00:08:28,083 --> 00:08:31,533
Run bash docker-shell.sh again:

135
00:08:31,533 --> 00:08:36,985
both files are there, yours
named ac215_2024.pem,

136
00:08:36,985 --> 00:08:40,392
and four AWS variables are set.

137
00:08:40,712 --> 00:08:42,889
Pause, and get to this prompt yourself.

138
00:08:45,033 --> 00:08:46,761
How was the toolbox started?

139
00:08:47,081 --> 00:08:49,705
Line 36 mounts the Docker socket.

140
00:08:50,025 --> 00:08:54,868
Then the deployment folder at /app,
your secrets, and the three app folders.

141
00:08:55,188 --> 00:09:00,009
Lines 43 to 46 hand in your keys
as environment variables.

142
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:01,253
A question.

143
00:09:01,573 --> 00:09:05,647
Inside the toolbox,
Ansible runs docker build for the frontend.

144
00:09:05,967 --> 00:09:10,298
Whose disk does the image land on:
the toolbox's, or your laptop's?

145
00:09:19,133 --> 00:09:20,242
Your laptop's.

146
00:09:20,562 --> 00:09:23,319
No Docker engine runs inside the toolbox;

147
00:09:23,319 --> 00:09:28,562
the Docker module of Ansible talks
through the socket to your laptop's Docker.

148
00:09:28,882 --> 00:09:32,381
So the image lands beside the toolbox,
not inside it.

149
00:09:32,701 --> 00:09:36,797
That's also why a prune,
run from inside, would sweep your laptop.

150
00:09:37,033 --> 00:09:41,056
Extra checks:
the AWS command line, Docker, kubectl

151
00:09:41,056 --> 00:09:42,791
and Ansible are there,

152
00:09:42,791 --> 00:09:46,292
but not the Ansible
the Dockerfile asked for.

153
00:09:46,533 --> 00:09:51,205
Without quotes, the greater than sign
in that pip install is a shell redirect.

154
00:09:51,525 --> 00:09:56,191
So the root folder holds a file
whose name starts with an equals sign:

155
00:09:56,191 --> 00:10:00,656
pip wrote its log there,
and installed Ansible 6, on an old Python.

156
00:10:00,976 --> 00:10:03,444
Quoting fixes it, but leave it for now:

157
00:10:03,444 --> 00:10:06,608
the provision playbook
relies on this old Ansible.

158
00:10:06,850 --> 00:10:10,371
That explains the warnings:
the old Python, a collection

159
00:10:10,371 --> 00:10:14,018
that doesn't support this Ansible,
and an old module name.

160
00:10:14,338 --> 00:10:15,874
We hide them from now on.

161
00:10:16,116 --> 00:10:21,513
aws sts get-caller-identity:
the security token is invalid.

162
00:10:21,833 --> 00:10:23,455
Ours is a placeholder.

163
00:10:23,775 --> 00:10:27,124
With your key,
this prints your account ID and your user.

164
00:10:29,466 --> 00:10:33,263
The inventory graph shows all,
then appserver, then one host.

165
00:10:33,583 --> 00:10:36,058
And all five playbooks pass a syntax check.

166
00:10:36,378 --> 00:10:37,872
The toolbox is ready.

167
00:10:39,017 --> 00:10:40,291
Step 4: in ECR,

168
00:10:40,291 --> 00:10:44,881
in us-east-1,
create three empty private repositories:

169
00:10:44,881 --> 00:10:49,321
the vector DB tool,
the API service and the frontend.

170
00:10:49,641 --> 00:10:52,564
Your policy can push to them,
but not create them.

171
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:56,640
The first playbook runs on localhost,
inside the toolbox.

172
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:59,413
Task 1 logs Docker in to ECR.

173
00:10:59,733 --> 00:11:03,318
Task 2 stamps a tag from the date,
down to the second,

174
00:11:03,318 --> 00:11:06,240
and registers it, so later tasks can use it.

175
00:11:06,484 --> 00:11:09,720
Then it builds the frontend
from /frontend-react,

176
00:11:09,720 --> 00:11:11,728
the folder the script mounted,

177
00:11:11,728 --> 00:11:14,740
names the image with that tag,
and pushes it.

178
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:18,111
The API and the vector DB tool follow.

179
00:11:18,431 --> 00:11:22,078
Last, it saves the tag in a file:
.docker-tag.

180
00:11:22,317 --> 00:11:28,205
Step 5: ansible-playbook
deploy-docker-images.yml -i inventory.yml.

181
00:11:28,525 --> 00:11:29,677
Pause and run it.

182
00:11:29,917 --> 00:11:35,336
Our first real error: at the ECR login,
the security token is invalid.

183
00:11:35,656 --> 00:11:40,669
The second line follows from the first:
docker login received an empty password.

184
00:11:40,989 --> 00:11:42,760
The recap says failed, 1.

185
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:44,963
Extra: no image was built.

186
00:11:45,283 --> 00:11:48,462
The laptop still holds only
the toolbox and its base.

187
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:53,447
With your keys, all 11 tasks run:
three images built on your laptop

188
00:11:53,447 --> 00:11:56,849
and pushed,
and your tag written to .docker-tag.

189
00:11:57,169 --> 00:11:59,474
Look for failed, zero, in the recap.

190
00:11:59,717 --> 00:12:02,682
The last task is docker system prune -a.

191
00:12:03,002 --> 00:12:06,992
Without -f, it asks, gets no answer,
and removes nothing.

192
00:12:07,312 --> 00:12:12,517
Every push stores three more images in ECR,
and nothing deletes old tags.

193
00:12:14,267 --> 00:12:19,408
Step 6 creates the server from localhost,
through the AWS API.

194
00:12:19,728 --> 00:12:25,542
First, a security group
with ports 22, 80 and 443 open to anyone,

195
00:12:25,542 --> 00:12:27,600
and a 50 gigabyte disk.

196
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:33,723
Then a t2.large instance with your key pair
and two 100 gigabyte disks of its own.

197
00:12:34,043 --> 00:12:37,286
Last, it tries
to attach the 50 gigabyte disk.

198
00:12:37,534 --> 00:12:39,265
From here, the meter runs:

199
00:12:39,265 --> 00:12:42,462
the instance by the hour
until you terminate it,

200
00:12:42,462 --> 00:12:44,894
its disks, and its public IP address.

201
00:12:45,934 --> 00:12:47,598
Pause and run step 6.

202
00:12:47,918 --> 00:12:50,616
Gathering facts works, then it fails:

203
00:12:50,616 --> 00:12:55,065
AWS was not able
to validate the provided access credentials.

204
00:12:55,385 --> 00:12:58,734
With real keys,
watch the untested attach task.

205
00:12:59,054 --> 00:13:03,512
If it fails, the server still works;
delete that spare disk when you clean up.

206
00:13:03,750 --> 00:13:09,233
A kind note: the heading says GCP;
it came over from the Google Cloud README.

207
00:13:09,467 --> 00:13:13,313
Step 7:
copy the public IP address of your instance

208
00:13:13,313 --> 00:13:18,064
from the EC2 console into the inventory,
under appserver hosts.

209
00:13:18,384 --> 00:13:20,176
Ours is a placeholder name.

210
00:13:20,417 --> 00:13:23,937
A small slip:
the key switch of step 9 is needed now.

211
00:13:24,257 --> 00:13:27,414
Pause, comment out line 4
and turn on line 7.

212
00:13:27,734 --> 00:13:34,561
It reads /secrets/ac215_2024.pem:
that exact name.

213
00:13:34,881 --> 00:13:37,484
The key on line 4 isn't in your toolbox.

214
00:13:37,734 --> 00:13:42,385
Step 8: pause and run ansible-playbook
deploy-provision-instance.yml.

215
00:13:42,705 --> 00:13:46,489
Unreachable:
could not resolve hostname EXTERNAL_IP,

216
00:13:46,489 --> 00:13:47,654
our placeholder.

217
00:13:47,974 --> 00:13:51,707
On yours, Ansible logs in as ubuntu,
with your key.

218
00:13:52,027 --> 00:13:53,729
Host key checking is off,

219
00:13:53,729 --> 00:13:56,998
so a new server is trusted blindly:
a trade-off.

220
00:13:57,234 --> 00:14:00,891
On a real server, 38 tasks install Docker,

221
00:14:00,891 --> 00:14:05,767
then format the second disk
and mount it at /mnt/disk-1.

222
00:14:06,087 --> 00:14:11,641
They install the AWS command
line and run aws configure with your keys:

223
00:14:11,641 --> 00:14:14,770
your badge now has a copy on the server.

224
00:14:16,517 --> 00:14:19,589
Step 9: the key switch, done before step 8.

225
00:14:20,217 --> 00:14:22,436
Two extras before you run step 10.

226
00:14:22,756 --> 00:14:26,082
The playbook starts Chroma
at the tag latest:

227
00:14:26,082 --> 00:14:28,744
at the time of recording, version 1,

228
00:14:28,744 --> 00:14:32,249
which answers the old API
with status 410, gone.

229
00:14:35,150 --> 00:14:39,400
The clients and the repo's compose
file pin an older version,

230
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:40,654
which answers 200.

231
00:14:40,974 --> 00:14:44,708
So change deploy-setup-containers.yml:151
to that version first.

232
00:14:46,934 --> 00:14:52,886
Second, the loader uses docker run -it,
which Docker refuses without a terminal.

233
00:14:53,206 --> 00:14:56,634
We couldn't test
whether Ansible provides one;

234
00:14:56,634 --> 00:14:58,795
if that task fails, drop -it.

235
00:14:59,034 --> 00:15:02,618
Step 10: ansible-playbook
deploy-setup-containers.yml.

236
00:15:02,938 --> 00:15:04,090
Pause and run it.

237
00:15:04,410 --> 00:15:09,658
Ours is unreachable, for the same reason;
here is what it does on a real server.

238
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:15,323
It writes your keys to a file only root
can open, a second copy on the server,

239
00:15:15,323 --> 00:15:19,564
and hands them to the API container
as environment variables.

240
00:15:19,884 --> 00:15:22,783
It creates a Docker network, appnetwork,

241
00:15:22,783 --> 00:15:26,626
and two folders on the data disk,
and logs in to ECR.

242
00:15:26,867 --> 00:15:28,537
Then it picks the tag:

243
00:15:28,537 --> 00:15:32,788
a DOCKER_TAG variable
in the toolbox's environment wins;

244
00:15:32,788 --> 00:15:35,187
otherwise, the .docker-tag file.

245
00:15:35,507 --> 00:15:37,406
From your laptop, it's the file.

246
00:15:37,650 --> 00:15:39,592
It pulls the three images

247
00:15:39,592 --> 00:15:43,943
and starts the frontend
on port 3000 and Chroma on 8000.

248
00:15:44,263 --> 00:15:47,914
A throwaway container
fills the vector database,

249
00:15:47,914 --> 00:15:50,045
then the API starts on 9000.

250
00:15:50,365 --> 00:15:52,776
The security group keeps those ports shut.

251
00:15:53,017 --> 00:15:55,556
Step 11: look around on the server.

252
00:15:55,876 --> 00:15:58,753
Where the README says GCP console,

253
00:15:58,753 --> 00:16:03,577
use SSH as ubuntu with your key,
or EC2 Instance Connect.

254
00:16:03,897 --> 00:16:07,204
Then list containers,
follow logs, or open a shell.

255
00:16:07,450 --> 00:16:10,031
Here they are on our laptop, as a stand-in.

256
00:16:10,351 --> 00:16:13,253
The image column carries the tag:
check it there.

257
00:16:13,573 --> 00:16:16,858
The nginx lines work
only after the next step.

258
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:25,171
The lecture's routing: one address,
/ to the frontend, /api/ to the API.

259
00:16:25,491 --> 00:16:30,717
Here the same shape sits on one machine,
and the ingress becomes an nginx container.

260
00:16:30,967 --> 00:16:35,511
Step 12 says create nginx.conf;
it's already in the repo.

261
00:16:35,831 --> 00:16:37,538
It listens on port 80.

262
00:16:37,858 --> 00:16:43,059
Paths that start with /api/
go to the API service on 9000;

263
00:16:43,059 --> 00:16:47,095
everything else goes
to the frontend on 3000.

264
00:16:47,333 --> 00:16:52,112
Predict:
the browser asks nginx for /api/newsletters.

265
00:16:52,432 --> 00:16:54,992
Which path does the API service receive?

266
00:16:55,312 --> 00:16:57,659
Look at the very end of line 37.

267
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:07,844
/newsletters.

268
00:17:08,164 --> 00:17:12,772
The trailing slash
after port 9000 replaces the matched prefix.

269
00:17:13,092 --> 00:17:15,980
Our stand-ins on the laptop confirm it,

270
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:19,535
and /api without its last slash
gets a redirect.

271
00:17:21,433 --> 00:17:24,486
The frontend calls /api on the same host,

272
00:17:24,486 --> 00:17:28,879
and the API mounts its routes
without the prefix: they fit.

273
00:17:29,233 --> 00:17:33,990
Step 13: pause and run ansible-playbook
deploy-setup-webserver.yml.

274
00:17:34,310 --> 00:17:35,654
Unreachable again.

275
00:17:35,974 --> 00:17:39,087
On a real server, it copies the config,

276
00:17:39,087 --> 00:17:43,079
starts nginx on ports 80 and 443,
and restarts it.

277
00:17:43,317 --> 00:17:46,794
Step 14:
open your server's address in a browser.

278
00:17:47,114 --> 00:17:48,992
Only port 80 does the work.

279
00:17:49,312 --> 00:17:53,046
Port 443 is open and forwarded to nginx,

280
00:17:53,046 --> 00:17:57,247
but nginx serves nothing there:
no HTTPS yet.

281
00:17:57,483 --> 00:18:01,664
That's part 1: about a dozen commands
and five playbooks, by hand.

282
00:18:01,984 --> 00:18:04,991
If you stop here, terminate the instance

283
00:18:04,991 --> 00:18:09,427
and delete its 50 gigabyte volume
in the EC2 console today:

284
00:18:09,427 --> 00:18:13,035
the README's delete step fails,
as part 2 shows.

285
00:18:13,355 --> 00:18:16,086
Next, a commit message does the redeploy.

